---
import Docs from "../layouts/Docs.astro";
import DocSection from "../components/docs/DocSection.astro";
import CommandBlock from "../components/docs/CommandBlock.astro";
import Callout from "../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import { block } from "../components/docs/commands";

const REPO = "https://github.com/nathansso/agentic_resume_tailoring";

// One list drives both the table of contents and the section headings.
const S = {
  prerequisites: { id: "prerequisites", title: "Before you start" },
  claude: { id: "claude-code", title: "Install in Claude Code" },
  codex: { id: "codex", title: "Install in Codex" },
  manual: { id: "other-clients", title: "Other MCP clients" },
  verify: { id: "check-it-worked", title: "Check that it worked" },
  github: { id: "github", title: "Bring in your GitHub repos (optional)" },
  optional: { id: "optional", title: "Optional extras" },
  jev: { id: "jev", title: "Optional: strengthen the checks with Jev" },
  trouble: { id: "troubleshooting", title: "Troubleshooting" },
};
const toc = Object.values(S);
---

<Docs
  title="Install: ARTie"
  description="Install ARTie in Claude Code or Codex in a few minutes: what you need first, the install commands, how to check it worked, and what to do if it doesn't."
  heading="Install"
  lede="ARTie is a plugin for your coding agent. Install it once and your agent can tailor your resume to a job posting from facts you have approved."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Callout title="New to MCP?">
    <p>
      MCP (Model Context Protocol) is how a coding agent such as Claude Code or Codex plugs into
      outside tools. The ARTie plugin sets up an MCP server named <code>artie</code> for you, and
      your agent calls its tools for you. You never call them yourself.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <DocSection section={S.prerequisites}>
    <p>You need two things on your computer, and ARTie's setup can install the second for you.</p>

    <h3>1. A coding agent: Claude Code or Codex</h3>
    <p>
      Follow the official install page for the one you use: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/setup" rel="noopener">Claude
      Code</a> or <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex" rel="noopener">Codex</a>. Claude
      Code is the recommended path. Both run in a terminal.
    </p>

    <h3>2. uv</h3>
    <p>
      <a href="https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/" rel="noopener">uv</a> is
      a Python tool runner. ARTie runs through <code>uvx</code>, which comes with uv, so you do
      not install Python yourself. You can skip this step: if uv is missing, ARTie's setup
      (<code>/artie:setup</code> in Claude Code, <code>$artie-setup</code> in Codex) notices, asks
      you, and runs uv's official installer for you. To install it yourself, the official page
      has the installer for each system, the same lines as these:
    </p>
    <CommandBlock label="macOS and Linux" lines={block("uv-install-unix")} />
    <CommandBlock label="Windows (PowerShell)" lines={block("uv-install-windows")} />
    <p>
      When it is done, open a new terminal and run <code>uv --version</code> to confirm.
    </p>

    <h3>No LaTeX to install</h3>
    <p>
      ARTie writes your resume in LaTeX. The first time it renders a PDF on a machine with no
      LaTeX engine, it downloads a pinned <a href="https://tectonic-typesetting.github.io" rel="noopener">Tectonic</a>,
      checks its checksum and keeps it in <code>~/.art/bin</code>. A tectonic or
      <code>pdflatex</code> already on your <code>PATH</code> is used instead. Set
      <code>ART_NO_DOWNLOAD=1</code> to turn the download off; you still get the
      <code>.tex</code> file.
    </p>

    <h3>Notes for your system</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>Windows</dt>
      <dd>
        Claude Code runs ARTie's hooks with Git Bash, or PowerShell if Git Bash is missing.
        Codex runs them with the session's shell, which is PowerShell by default. If an install
        fails on a long file path, see <a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a>.
      </dd>
      <dt>macOS and Linux</dt>
      <dd>Nothing extra. Hooks run with <code>sh</code>.</dd>
    </dl>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.claude}>
    <p>
      This is the recommended route. Open a terminal, start <code>claude</code>, and enter these
      two commands inside Claude Code:
    </p>
    <CommandBlock label="In Claude Code" lines={block("claude-plugin")} />
    <p>
      The first adds ARTie's plugin catalog. The second installs the plugin, which registers
      the MCP server, the <code>/artie:*</code> commands and the hooks in one step.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first start downloads ARTie and takes longer than later ones. It needs the network
      once, then reuses what it downloaded.
    </p>
    <h3>No uv yet? Run the setup</h3>
    <p>
      Run <code>/artie:setup</code>. If ARTie's tools don't answer, it finds out why before
      anything else. When uv is missing it names uv's official installer and waits for your
      yes, then offers to install the <code>artie</code> command from the same release the plugin
      runs, which also puts <code>artie</code> on your <code>PATH</code>. Nothing is installed
      without your say-so; if you decline, it prints the line to run yourself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then restart ARTie once, the way the setup tells you: type <code>/mcp</code> and reconnect
      the <code>artie</code> server (or start Claude Code again from a new terminal), or quit and
      reopen the desktop app. Run <code>/artie:setup</code> again and it carries on with your
      resume. In Codex, <code>$artie-setup</code> does the same, and you restart Codex.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.codex}>
    <p>The same plugin works in Codex. Run these two commands in a terminal:</p>
    <CommandBlock label="In a terminal" lines={block("codex-plugin")} />
    <p>
      The plugin carries ARTie's hooks, which keep your pinned preferences in front of the agent
      and tell it what you changed by hand in the editor. Codex 0.131 and later load them, and
      skip them until you trust them: run <code>/hooks</code> inside Codex once after
      installing, and again after an upgrade changes them.
    </p>

    <h3>Older Codex, or Codex without the plugin</h3>
    <p>
      If your Codex is older than 0.131, or you register only the server (see <a href="#other-clients">Other MCP clients</a>), install the hooks into your own <code>hooks.json</code> instead:
    </p>
    <CommandBlock label="In a terminal" lines={block("codex-hooks")} />
    <p>
      Then run <code>/hooks</code> to review and trust them. With the plugin installed this
      command adds nothing and removes ARTie's own entries from <code>hooks.json</code>, so no
      hook runs twice. Without the plugin, run it again after you upgrade ARTie: it replaces
      ARTie's earlier hooks and leaves your own alone.
    </p>

    <h3>Approving writes</h3>
    <p>
      ARTie's write tools (the ones that save to your resume library or render a file) ask for
      approval under Codex's default policy. Approve them, or choose to always allow the <code>artie</code> server.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.manual}>
    <p>
      If you use another MCP client, or want only the tools without the plugin, register the
      server by hand. For Claude Code:
    </p>
    <CommandBlock label="In a terminal" lines={block("claude-mcp-add")} />
    <p>For Codex:</p>
    <CommandBlock label="In a terminal" lines={block("codex-mcp-add")} />
    <p>
      For any other client that starts MCP servers over stdio, the server is everything after
      the <code>--</code> in those lines: the command <code>uvx</code> with the arguments that
      follow it.
    </p>
    <Callout title="What you give up">
      <p>
        A by-hand registration gives your agent ARTie's tools only. The <code>/artie:*</code> commands,
        the skills that teach the agent the workflow, and the hooks come with the plugin.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <p>
      The install runs <code>artie mcp</code>, which is the same server as <code>artie-mcp</code>.
      Use <code>artie mcp</code>: a machine's Application Control policy may block one executable
      and not the other.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.verify}>
    <p>
      The first time it starts, ARTie creates its local store at <code>~/.art/art.db</code> and
      a default profile. Your data stays in that local file; ARTie keeps no copy on a server.
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>See that the server is connected.</strong> In Claude Code, type <code>/mcp</code> and
        find the entry for <code>artie</code>. In Codex, ask the agent in the next step.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ask the agent for ARTie's tools.</strong> Say: "List the ART tools you can use."
        The answer should include <code>art_briefing</code>, <code>open_job</code>, <code>execute_plan</code> and <code>render</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ask what it has saved.</strong> Say: "What preferences does ARTie have for me?"
        (the agent calls <code>art_briefing</code>). On a fresh install the answer is none, and that
        is a good result: it means the agent reached ARTie.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      You can also check from a terminal, without any agent. This prints every tool as JSON:
    </p>
    <CommandBlock label="In a terminal" lines={block("list-tools")} />
    <p>If any of these fail, see <a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a>.</p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.github}>
    <p>
      Skip this if you would rather clone your repositories and hand your agent the folders (see
      the <a href="/usage">Usage page</a>). Sign in with GitHub once and your agent can list
      your repositories itself and choose which to read.
    </p>
    <p>
      Run this in your own terminal, not through your agent. It prints a code and a GitHub
      page; open the page, type the code and approve. Your password never goes through ARTie.
    </p>
    <CommandBlock label="In a terminal" lines={block("github-login")} />
    <p>
      The first line reads your public repositories. The second, with <code>--private</code>,
      also reads private ones. Run whichever you want; you can run the other later.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>/artie:setup</code> puts the <code>artie</code> command on your <code>PATH</code>. If
      your terminal says it doesn't know <code>artie</code>, run the same lines through
      <code>uvx</code>:
    </p>
    <CommandBlock label="In a terminal" lines={block("github-login-uvx")} />
    <p>
      Nothing else needs installing: the <code>keyring</code> package ships with ARTie, and the
      token it gets is kept in your operating system's keychain (Windows Credential Manager,
      macOS Keychain, or your desktop's secret service). It is never stored in ARTie's
      database, never included in an export, and never logged.
    </p>
    <p>
      On a machine with no usable keychain, such as some headless Linux setups, the login says
      so before it starts. Set <code>ART_GITHUB_TOKEN</code> to a GitHub personal access token
      in the environment your agent starts from instead.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.optional}>
    <p>ARTie works fully without any of this. Add what you want, later.</p>

    <h3>Extras</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt><code>[ui]</code></dt>
      <dd>
        <code>artie ui</code>, a local editor where you adjust a tailored resume by hand. The
        plugin's install already includes it.
      </dd>
      <dt><code>[pdf]</code></dt>
      <dd>Document readers (docling, pypdf, python-docx).</dd>
      <dt><code>[embed]</code></dt>
      <dd>Sentence-transformers for semantic matching.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      The walkthrough on the <a href="/usage">Usage page</a> needs none of them. The default
      install is deliberately light: no torch, no model clients.
    </p>

    <h3>The local editor: artie ui</h3>
    <p>
      The release ships with the editor built in, so you need no checkout, Node or <code>npm</code>.
      Fill in the job's id (your agent can tell you, or run <code>/artie:open</code> in Claude
      Code and it prints this line for you):
    </p>
    <CommandBlock label="In a terminal" lines={block("art-ui")} />
    <p>
      It opens at <code>http://127.0.0.1:8765/</code>, on your machine only. Anything you save
      there comes back to your agent on your next message.
    </p>

  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.jev}>
    <p>
      ARTie works fully without this, so skip it if you like. If you have a TypeSafe API key,
      ARTie can use it, from your computer with no ARTie server in between, to ask <a href="https://typesafe.ai/blog/introducing-system-one-models-and-jev" rel="noopener">TypeSafe's
      Jev</a> a few narrow yes/no and multiple-choice questions that plain rules answer less
      well:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Does a reworded bullet say more than its cited source supports?</li>
      <li>Does a bullet mention a topic you banned, in other words?</li>
      <li>Which of your saved bullets fits a job best?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Jev never writes resume text. Without a key, each question falls back to a plain rule
      (a check of the bullet's wording against its cited source, a literal term match, word
      overlap, or your agent asking you), and those rules still gate your resume. Jev adds the
      reworded cases they miss.
    </p>
    <p>
      To turn it on, set the key in the environment Claude Code or Codex starts from, for
      example in your shell profile:
    </p>
    <CommandBlock label="Shell profile (macOS, Linux, Git Bash)" lines={block("typesafe-key")} />
    <p>
      On Windows, set it as a user environment variable named <code>TYPESAFE_API_KEY</code>, the
      same way as <code>UV_CACHE_DIR</code> under <a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a>.
      Then restart your agent.
    </p>
    <p>
      The key is never logged or stored in the database. When a key is set and an answer is not
      already cached, a small piece of text goes to <code>api.typesafe.ai</code>: the bullet or
      message in question and the posting requirements it is checked against, never your whole
      resume or the whole posting. To be certain nothing is sent, leave the key unset or set <code>ART_JEV_MODE=off</code>.
      To see whether ARTie can see a key, run the check command from above with <code>artie jev
      status</code> in place of <code>artie --list</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full list of what is sent is in <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/INSTALL.md`} rel="noopener">INSTALL.md</a>.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.trouble}>
    <h3>The first start is slow</h3>
    <p>
      The first start downloads the release and builds its environment. That needs the network
      and takes a while. After that, uv keeps both in its cache. ARTie's hooks do nothing until
      the server has started once, so the first message you send may get no hook output and the
      next one will.
    </p>

    <h3>It fails to start when you are offline</h3>
    <p>
      uv re-checks its cached answers a few minutes after they were made. With the network on,
      that costs a second or two. With it off, a start after the cache has aged fails once uv
      gives up retrying, about 15 seconds. There are two fixes, both using what is already
      cached.
    </p>
    <p>
      Set this in the environment Claude Code or Codex starts from. uv then never touches the
      network and uses the cache as it is (after one earlier online start):
    </p>
    <CommandBlock label="Environment variable" lines={block("uv-offline")} />
    <p>Or install ARTie as a uv tool once, so uvx stops re-resolving it:</p>
    <CommandBlock label="In a terminal" lines={block("uv-tool-install")} />
    <p>Upgrade it later by installing the next release the same way.</p>

    <h3>Windows: the install fails on a long path</h3>
    <p>
      uv's default cache folder on Windows can be nested deep enough to break the install. Point
      uv at a short one before you start Claude Code or Codex. In PowerShell:
    </p>
    <CommandBlock label="PowerShell" lines={block("uv-cache-dir")} />
    <p>
      Programs read it when they start, so open a new terminal and start your agent again.
    </p>

    <h3>Windows: Application Control blocks an executable</h3>
    <p>
      If a policy on your machine blocks <code>artie-mcp.exe</code>, the plugin is unaffected,
      because it runs <code>artie mcp</code>. If you registered the server by hand, use <code>artie
      mcp</code> as in <a href="#other-clients">Other MCP clients</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Codex hooks do nothing on Windows</h3>
    <p>
      The hook line needs a PowerShell or bash session shell. If Codex has none and falls back
      to <code>cmd.exe</code>, <code>;</code> is not a separator there and the hook exits
      without doing anything. Use a PowerShell or bash session.
    </p>

    <h3>You get a .tex file but no PDF</h3>
    <p>
      ARTie found no LaTeX engine and could not download Tectonic: the render's hint says why
      (offline, or <code>ART_NO_DOWNLOAD=1</code>). Connect and render again, or install
      tectonic or <code>pdflatex</code> on your <code>PATH</code>.
    </p>

    <Callout title="Still stuck?">
      <p>
        Open an issue on <a href={`${REPO}/issues`} rel="noopener">GitHub</a> with the command
        you ran and what it printed.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </DocSection>
</Docs>
